Apple's HomeView is out today, and we're finally learning all the little details about the new hub. We've rounded up a list of tidbits for those setting up a HomeView or thinking about getting one.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/homeview-lifestyle.jpg" alt="" width="1600" height="900" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1070346" /><br/>
<h2>Sunglasses</h2><br/>
Apple says the HomeView probably won't recognize you if you have your face covered, including when wearing sunglasses. If HomeView isn't identifying you, Apple suggests removing anything that would block the camera from seeing your face.<br/>
<br/>
The HomeView's screen needs to be angled toward your face, you need to be centered in the frame and closest to the camera if other people are around, and the HomeView can't be blocked by overhanging shelves and cabinets. Apple says the HomeView needs front-facing, even light.<br/>
<h2>Mirror</h2><br/>
There's a "Mirror" in the HomeView Control Center that lets you use the camera to see yourself. There's an option to turn on an edge light for face illumination, along with 1x and 2x zoom.<br/>
<h2>Guest Controls</h2><br/>
The HomeView owner has multiple options for what guests can access.<br/>
<ul><br/>
 	<li>Safari can be turned on or off, or adult websites can be blocked.</li><br/>
 	<li>By default, guests can use <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/siri/">Siri</a> for general knowledge, writing help, and math help, but &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; features can be turned off.</li><br/>
 	<li>Explicit content can be turned on or off.</li><br/>
 	<li>There's an option to choose which <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-music/">Apple Music</a> subscription supplies music for an unrecognized user.</li><br/>
</ul><br/>
<h2>Wi-Fi/Cellular</h2><br/>
The HomeView authenticates people through face recognition and by making sure their iOS device is nearby, recently unlocked, and on the same Wi-Fi network as the HomeView. If there's a Wi-Fi outage, Apple says cellular can also be used to unlock personal content as long as it's in the same location and was recently on the Wi-Fi network.<br/>
<h2>Sleep Timer</h2><br/>
You can set a sleep timer on HomeView to pause whatever media is playing when the timer goes off.<br/>
<h2>No Buttons</h2><br/>
There is no button to turn the HomeView on or off, or restart it. It can be restarted from the Settings app or by briefly unplugging it.<br/>
<h2>Logins</h2><br/>
You can log into websites and apps using your iPhone, similar to how the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/apple-tv/">Apple TV</a> works.<br/>
<h2>Notifications</h2><br/>
The HomeView only has notifications for phone calls and home devices, with an option to toggle on critical notifications. There are no other notification settings or options.<br/>
<h2>Liquid Glass</h2><br/>
The transparency of the Liquid Glass interface can be adjusted from clear to opaque like on iPhone, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a>, and Mac.<br/>
<h2>Personal Data Lockouts</h2><br/>
